"A piano has one string for each note in the bass, two for each note in the tenor, and three for each note in the treble. They are steel wire, and the bass strings are wound with copper. A string alone is not loud, because a thin string moves little air. Its vibrations go through a bridge to a large soundboard that moves a lot of air. When you let go of the key, the damper falls back on the string, and the sound stops. The strings of a modern grand pull with a total of more than 20 tons.": "b495006c4af0",
"A piano is a keyboard instrument that makes sound when its keys are pressed, which activates an action where hammers strike strings. A modern piano usually has 88 keys: 52 white and 36 black, from A0 to C8. In the Hornbostel–Sachs system it is a chordophone, because strings are what vibrate, and it is played by striking. The name piano is short for pianoforte, from an Italian phrase for a harpsichord with soft and loud.": "15ac2932733c",
"A piano is a keyboard instrument that makes sound when its keys are pressed, which activates an action where hammers strike strings. A modern piano usually has 88 keys: 52 white and 36 black, from A0 to C8. In the Hornbostel–Sachs system it is a chordophone, because strings are what vibrate, and it is played by striking. The name piano is short for pianoforte, from an Italian phrase for a harpsichord with soft and loud. That name matters. On a harpsichord a note sounds equally loud no matter how hard you press the key. The piano changed that. The harder you press, the faster the hammer moves and the louder the sound. From pianissimo to fortissimo, the hammer speed changes by almost a factor of a hundred. The harder blow is also brighter: the contact between the hammer and the string is shorter, and a shorter contact puts more high partials into the sound. Between your finger and the string is the action, a mechanical assembly that turns the pressing of a key into the rapid motion of a hammer. It amplifies a small movement of the key into a longer, faster movement of the hammer. In a grand piano the rising key lifts a part called the wippen, the wippen carries a jack, and the jack pushes the hammer up toward the string. At the same time, the key lifts the damper, a felt block that normally rests on the string and keeps it still. Here is the clever part. The hammer must strike the string but not stay on it, because continued contact would damp the sound. So just before the hammer reaches the string, the jack is tripped and slips away. This is called let-off, and it frees the hammer. The hammer flies on by its own momentum, strikes, and rebounds, and a back check catches it. The contact lasts only about 4 thousandths of a second at pianissimo, and under 2 at fortissimo. A piano has one string for each note in the bass, two for each note in the tenor, and three for each note in the treble. They are steel wire, and the bass strings are wound with copper. A string alone is not loud, because a thin string moves little air. Its vibrations go through a bridge to a large soundboard that moves a lot of air. When you let go of the key, the damper falls back on the string, and the sound stops. The strings of a modern grand pull with a total of more than 20 tons. The pedals change what the dampers and hammers do. The right pedal, the damper pedal, lifts all the dampers, so notes keep ringing after you release the keys. It does not make notes louder, even though it is sometimes called the loud pedal. On a grand piano the left pedal shifts the whole action so the hammers hit two of the three strings, and that changes the timbre as well as the loudness. And the strings are a little stiff, so their overtones are slightly sharp. That is why tuners tune octaves slightly wider than 2 to 1.": "4a009e20c806",
